## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical webhook security beyond basic authentication, focusing on signature verification, key rotation, replay protection, and incident response readiness.

## Key Concepts
- **Webhook signing and verification** using HMAC or JWS standards
- **Key rotation** with per-provider scoping to limit blast radius
- **Replay protection** via nonce and timestamp validation
- **Idempotent processing** with deduplication keys to handle duplicate events
- **Edge validation** at the gateway layer for early rejection
- **Incident response readiness** with documented playbooks and success criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');

function verifySignature(payload, sig, key, ts, nonce, seenNonces, windowSeconds = 300) {
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  const timestamp = parseInt(ts, 10);

  if (Math.abs(now - timestamp) > windowSeconds) {
    throw new Error('Timestamp outside valid window');
  }

  if (seenNonces.has(nonce)) {
    throw new Error('Nonce already seen - replay detected');
  }
  seenNonces.add(nonce);

  const expectedSig = crypto
    .createHmac('sha256', key)
    .update(`${ts}.${nonce}.${payload}`)
    .digest('hex');

  if (!crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(sig), Buffer.from(expectedSig))) {
    throw new Error('Invalid signature');
  }

  return true;
}

function idempotentHandler(eventId, handler) {
  const lock = redis.lock(`event:${eventId}`);
  if (!lock.acquire()) return { status: 'already_processing' };

  try {
    const result = cache.get(`processed:${eventId}`);
    if (result) return { status: 'duplicate', result };

    const output = handler();
    cache.set(`processed:${eventId}`, output, { ttl: 86400 });
    return { status: 'processed', result: output };
  } finally {
    lock.release();
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of CORS risks and practical mitigations in a fintech SPA context, focusing on per-origin controls, token usage, and incident response.

## Key Concepts
- CORS headers and policies
- Token-based auth and JWTs
- CI/CD security checks for config
- Incident response for cross-origin abuse

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example snippet for server CORS config (pseudo)
const allowedOrigins = ['https://app.example.com','https://admin.app.example.com'];
app.use((req,res,next)=>{ 
  const origin = req.headers.origin;
  if (allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) { res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', origin);
     res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials','true');
     res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods','GET,POST,OPTIONS');
  } else { res.status(403).send('origin not allowed'); }
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate detection of misconfig in CI?
- How would you validate that CORS policies don’t block legitimate cross-origin traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests ability to design practical, compliant data access controls across serverless and containerized services, with ABAC, key management, RBAC, policy-as-code, and IR planning.

## Key Concepts
- ABAC attributes and enforcement points
- MongoDB Atlas RBAC & Client-Side Encryption
- AWS KMS key management and rotation
- Policy-as-code (OPA) integration and CI gates
- Incident response timing and evidence collection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-authorization check example
function hasAccess(attrs, request) {
  const {service, tenant, data_class, operation, region} = attrs;
  const r = request;
  return r.service === service && r.tenant === tenant && r.data_class === data_class && r.operation === operation && r.region === region;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test ABAC policies in CI/CD and simulate breaches?
- How would you audit data access for forensics and regulatory reporting?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to apply fundamental security controls to third-party components—a common real-world risk at many companies. It evaluates your understanding of vendor vetting, data minimization, privacy controls, and incident response readiness.

## Key Concepts
- Third-party risk management
- Data minimization and PII redaction
- User consent and privacy controls
- Observability and incident response readiness

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: redact PII from analytics payload
function redact(payload) {
  const redacted = { ...payload };
  if (redacted.userEmail) redacted.userEmail = "[REDACTED]";
  if (redacted.userPhone) redacted.userPhone = "[REDACTED]";
  return redacted;
}

// Example: hash identifiers before transmission
function hashIdentifier(identifier) {
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(identifier).digest('hex');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical application of phishing-resistant MFA and device-based access in admin contexts, plus rollout, exceptions, and measurable outcomes.

## Key Concepts

- phishing-resistant MFA (FIDO2/WebAuthn)
- passwordless SSO
- device posture (MDM/EMM)
- conditional access rules
- centralized logging (SIEM) and phishing simulations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example simple authorization gate
function allowAccess(user, device) {
  const okUser = user.isAdmin;
  const okDevice = device.isCompliant;
  const okMFA = user.mfaCompletedWithFido2;
  return okUser && okDevice && okMFA;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle a lost FIDO key or key compromise?
- What metrics would you track to gauge phishing-resistant MFA effectiveness?
